Issue 2-3-3: Considerations on Top-level structuring The NR 38.133 spec structure is borrowed from LTE 36.133, and the key difference for baseline sections are: (1) split signal characteristics from TS36.133 into two sections signal characteristics and timing from TS38.133. (2)removing the measurements performance requirements of network. We believe the current TS38.133 structure is stable and efficient for all possible RRM functions and the additional dedicated function can be added into sub-sections with specific suffix. We prefer to reuse the same main structure or top-level skeleton from 5G RRM spec to 6G, however, RAN4 shall be open to modify it later if any key technical change is observed for RRM scope extension or reduction. Reuse the same top-level skeleton from 5G RRM spec to 6G; however, RAN4 shall be open to modify it later if any key technical change is observed for RRM scope extension or reduction. Issue 2-3-4: Considerations for lower level structuring Regarding the low-level skeleton of the RRM spec, we think it can be discussed in the WI stage or at least at the late stage of this SI when we see more clear scope of the day1 features in 6G RRM. Otherwise, it’s hard to decide what the exact section structure relying on RRM features can be adopted. For low-level skeleton of the RRM spec, it can be discussed in the WI stage or at least at the late stage of this SI when we see more clear scope of the day1 features in 6G RRM.

